<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian indenture system</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1917">1917</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as girmit</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian indenture system was a system of indentured servitude that carried more than 1.6 million workers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to European colonies after the abolition of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Described by colonial authorities as free migration, it relied on recruits who were often deceived, coerced or kidnapped, and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-tinker">Hugh Tinker</a> called it a new form of slavery. It began with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlas-mountains">Atlas</a> voyage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mauritius">Mauritius</a> in 1834 and spread to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, Natal, Réunion and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>, where labourers worked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantations under harsh discipline and criminal penalties. Export to Natal ended in 1911 and to the other colonies in 1917, leaving large Indian diasporas.</p></div>
